      "claim": "In mice, platensimycin showed low brain exposure with plasma-to-brain ratios of 19.4x, 31.2x, and 4.7x at 0.5, 1, and 3 h after oral dosing, indicating inadequate CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C07"],
      "claim": "In dogs, sulbenicillin given as a 30 mg/kg intravenous injection produced almost no detectable cerebrospinal fluid concentration, supporting poor CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C18"],
      "claim": "In two human subjects, [11C]AZ10419369 was rapidly distributed into the brain after intravenous injection, with 3-4% of injected radioactivity present in the brain at peak, indicating meaningful CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Most transferred in-vivo records show low brain, CSF, or brain-to-plasma exposure after systemic administration, supporting a fail prediction. A few positive PET and brain-cyst access records provide contradictory evidence, but the overall direct systemic exposure pattern favors inadequate CNS access.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct query-specific CNS exposure record is available; prediction relies on transferred evidence.",
    "Some positive records are radiotracer-based or qualitative and may not reflect therapeutic CNS exposure.",
    "Species and route differences across records limit direct comparability."
  ],
